A: Try to use the contract assistant in VS2010.
As the error states, you are using the contract attribute in a member that does not support contracts. This typically happens in properties, fields, or return types by default. Contract attributes such as ContractInvariantMethod and ContractTerminatingMethod can only be used in methods.
To resolve this, you just need to move this attribute to a method of ArrayList or to the 'Add' function for instance. 

A: Unfortunately, the contract requirements can't be checked by the visual studio's contract assistant. The contract attributes are used to express contracts about the behavior of a method, but they must be applied to methods, not properties or fields. In addition, contracts can only be used at the level of methods that can be called, not properties, fields, or classes or modules as a whole.
So, the ContractClassAttribute, ContractInvariantMethod, and ContractTerminatingMethod attributes can only be used with the methods of the class, not with the properties, fields or as a whole class or module. 
Unfortunately, the Contract Class cannot be applied to properties, fields, or static members because they are not designed to be invoked from outside the class they are declared in.
These are built-in attributes in C# where the language does not allow these attributes to be applied to some types of members. If these requirements are applicable to your code, then you should use Contract Class attributes in a way that is consistent with the rules and restrictions of the language. 
Hope the Visual Studio Contract Assistant does not support these attributes...
Hopefully, the attributes can be used on methods because it is consistent with the typical usage of these attributes.
To check the behavior of the method, you need to manually check it.
This can be verified manually by checking whether every method invocation of the method complies with the contract.
To manually verify a method is compliant, you need to:
• Check that the method has the correct return type.
• Ensure any parameters the method has are used correctly.
• Check whether every possible scenario is handled.
• For loops or switch statements, ensure that break or return is used.
• Check every statement in the method to ensure parameters are used properly.
• If possible, simulate the scenario to verify the method behavior.
Hope this helps...

The key thing to remember here is that the framework Contract class only applies to executable code (methods), not to declarations.

The Contract Assistant is designed to discover issues early in the software development process and can be used, for example, to check the correctness and completeness of a method contract as part of unit testing. Contracts are used to ensure that code meets certain conditions and behaviors that should always be true. For example, suppose a method states that it throws an ArgumentException if a certain condition is not met, then a Contract Assistant could help to find places in the code where that condition might not be met as early in the development process as a typical unit test might. After a correct implementation, the Contract Assistant could help to identify any places in the code where the condition for the exception might not actually be met. But at the point where a Contract Assistant will start reporting issues, those contracts will have already been checked by the compiler.

The rules for using contracts in C# are:


*

*Contract attributes can only be used on methods.

*Conditions in contracts are discharged at the end of a method (i.e., right before it returns).

*Contracts are not checked during partial evaluation (i.e., when code is compiled in a way that simplifies the method's behavior).

*Conditions in contracts can't throw.


A ContractException is thrown when a condition in a Contract.Requires, Contract.Assert, or Contract.Invariant is not met, whether the code is running in debug mode or not.
References :
1) Microsoft (R) .NET Framework Contract Aspects
.NET Framework Contract Aspects (2008)
2) Using Contracts in Microsoft.NET Framework
