}
```
Pex also generates a test framework for ensuring the correctness of your unit tests and it has additional features for creating stress tests and mutation testing. These features make it possible to generate a large number of random test inputs and to test how your code behaves under different conditions and to check whether your code has any potential bugs or flaws.

**Testing without Mocks:**
The problem with mocks, is that they often end up with very little valuable testing. In other words, even though you can find no bugs in your mock code, it doesn’t mean that the functionality it was mocking correctly. This is because a mock represents an isolated and fragile part of the system. If the mock isn’t correctly implemented, the system as a whole can behave in ways that are hard to predict.
Instead of mocking, consider writing integration tests that require both the unit of code being tested and some external system. By including the external system in the test, you ensure that the test also includes the real dependencies of that unit. This test will not be as fast as a unit test could be, but the extra time spent on these integration tests is usually well-spent since they can detect behavior that would be difficult to replicate in a unit test - in fact, often impossible.

**Testing with Mock Frameworks:**
Most .NET developers mock interfaces (or classes that have methods that aren’t going to be called) to isolate the unit of code being tested from its collaborators. Some popular .NET mock-ing libraries include Moq, NSubstitute, Rhino Mocks, etc. These libraries allow you to easily create a mock for an interface, set up scenarios in which it will return particular values, verify that certain methods have been called, etc.
Many of these libraries provide ways for the developer to specify that a method should throw an exception (e.g., a "verification failure") when it is called. This can be used to test error handling logic. Moq, for instance, provides the Throws extension method for this purpose.
A popular .NET testing framework in recent years has been xUnit, and there’s a mocking library that integrates xUnit and Moq. This can be used to create a mock in an xUnit Theory method (which are effectively unit tests) and to test the behavior of code that uses the mock.

**Test Drive Development(TDD):**
Test-Driven Development (TDD) means that the developer writes a test before writing the code to fulfill that requirement. The process involves the following steps:
A test is written that fails, which forces the developer to think about the necessary code
The developer writes the simplest code that can make the test pass 
The developer refactors the code to clean up any ad-hoc solutions to the problem
The test is then moved to a more specific failing test which forces the developer to think about the necessary code for the second piece of functionality

**BDD (Behavior Driven Development):**
BDD is a software development technique; the central idea is that the behavior that you're specifying in your tests should be about the desired behavior of your system. In other words, you're writing tests that describe the behavior of your system.

**Code Contracts:**
Code contracts are techniques for detecting and understanding error-handling problems early in the development process. They encourage the early detection and understanding of possible errors, and serve as an additional safety check on the behavior of an application. Code contracts can be used for both routine error handling and assertion checking.

**Test Automation:**
Automated testing can save a lot of time, and can be done using frameworks like NUnit or MSTest. They can be set up in the system to run automatically whenever any change is made to the project. The test results can be displayed in real-time and can easily be collected and managed. Tools like Selenium and Cucumber can be used for automated UI testing.

The benefits of automated testing include:
Speed: Automated testing can often run faster because it doesn't have to go through the setup process each time you want to perform a test, it can perform multiple tests at once.
Reliability: Automated tests tend to be more reliable because they can be set up to repeat the same tests every time, increasing--------------------            // and New_Ret >= Old_arrListLastIndexOfX && New_Ret <= Old_arrListCount-1 => New_arrListContainsX
	    // an assertion using "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" that  if the return value is non-negative, x is in the new array 
            // && (New_Ret >= Old_arrListCount || New_Ret < 0) => New_arrListContainsX
	    // a postcondition attribute with condition (@@@) that (@@@) if New_Ret >= 0 => New_arrListContainsX
        }
    }
}
```

### <a name="delegates"></a>Entradas delegadas

Las entradas delegadas permiten validar los resultados de una operación en la lista a través de la comparación de resultados. Puede utilizar entradas delegadas para comprobar si la operación de lista ha modificado la lista de entrada para las entradas dadas. Esto es especialmente �til para comprobar si las operaciones de lista (añadir, quitar, ordenar, filtrar, etc.) han modificado la lista de entrada.

Por ejemplo, puede escribir pruebas de integración para la clase `Add` de `ArrayList`. Estas pruebas deben generar una lista de entrada y llamar a `Add` para que el código genere una lista de salida. A continuación, puede usar entradas delegadas para ejecutar una comprobación para asegurarse de que la lista de salida tiene un elemento extra en la posición especificada.

Otro ejemplo sencillo es comprobar que los parámetros de entrada dados se conservan en la lista. Puede usar entradas delegadas para probar esta propiedad. En primer lugar, use las entradas generadas por la prueba automática para llamar a la operación de lista (añadir, quitar, ordenar, etc.) en un parámetro `[PexMembers]`. Luego, compruebe que el parámetro de entrada no se ha modificado.

## <a name="conclusion"></a>Conclusión

Las herramientas para prueba de código de Microsoft.Pex y Pex Lib proveen una forma práctica de probar automáticamente las clases de .NET. Al probar mediante la generación de pruebas de "entrada" adecuadas y "salida" para una clase de .NET, puede aumentar la confianza en ella y descubrir errores de forma automatizada y eficiente. A medida que escribe las pruebas, estas se van automatizando para que puede centrarse en escribir pruebas unitarias y de integración en la lógica de la aplicación, en lugar de las pruebas automatizadas. También puede ayudar a mejorar la cobertura de código y la calidad del software.

